## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about live-code updates in a running kernel, including synchronization, cache coherence, and control of instruction fetch paths under contention.

## Key Concepts

- Live patching of executable memory without stopping workers
- Per-page state machines and atomic publish
- I-cache/TLB synchronization and rollback safety

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal C-like sketch (pseudo-code)
enum { STABLE, STAGED, ACTIVE } state_t;
typedef struct { void *addr; void *code; state_t s; volatile int refcnt; uint64_t ver; } page_t;

int publish_patch(page_t *p, void *new_code) {
  p->s = STAGED;
  p->code = new_code;
  __sync_synchronize();
  if (atomic_compare_exchange(&p->s, STAGED, ACTIVE)) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&p->ver, 1);
    icache_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    tlb_invalidate(p->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    return 0;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you guarantee rollback if a patch proves faulty?
- How to test latency under burst patch arrivals and mixed workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of concurrency in OS caches, race handling, and data integrity under contention.

## Key Concepts
- CAS-based locking and per-block loading state
- Wait queues and wakeups for concurrent readers
- Read-ahead decisions and eviction interactions

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch in C-like pseudo
typedef struct BlockEntry { inode_t *inode; int block_no; void *data; atomic_int loading; atomic_int ready; wait_queue_t wq; } BlockEntry;
BlockEntry *cache_lookup(inode_t*, int); void disk_read_async(inode_t*, int, void*);

void* get_block(inode_t *inode, int block_no) {
  BlockEntry *e = cache_lookup(inode, block_no);
  if (e && atomic_load(&e->ready)) return e->data;
  if (e == NULL) {
    e = allocate_entry(inode, block_no);
    if (atomic_compare_and_swap(&e->loading, 0, 1)) {
      disk_read_async(inode, block_no, e->data);
      atomic_store(&e->ready, 1);
      wake_all(&e->wq);
    } else {
      wait_event(&e->wq, atomic_load(&e->ready) == 1);
    }
  } else {
    wait_event(&e->wq, atomic_load(&e->ready) == 1);
  }
  return e->data;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify no duplicate disk reads occur under concurrent requests?
- How would you extend with proactive read-ahead and cache eviction rules?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of page replacement and trace-based fault analysis. It also checks ability to translate a policy into a tiny simulator and reason about edge cases in a beginner OS context.

## Key Concepts

- Page replacement policies (FIFO, CLOCK)
- Counting faults from a reference string
- Clock hand movement and per-frame reference bits

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal CLOCK eviction path (pseudo)
function clockVictim(frames, hand){
  while(true){
    if(!frames[hand].ref){
      const victim = hand;
      // evict frames[victim]
      return (victim + 1) % frames.length;
    } else {
      frames[hand].ref = false;
      hand = (hand + 1) % frames.length;
    }
  }
}
```

