## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic synchronization primitives and common OS patterns, especially producer-consumer, deadlock avoidance, and correct semaphore/mutex usage in C.

## Key Concepts
- Semaphores
- Mutex
- Circular buffer
- Deadlock detection
- Boundary conditions (full/empty)

## Code Example
```c
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>
#include <stdio.h>

#define N 10

int buffer[N];
int in = 0, out = 0;
sem_t empty, full;
pthread_mutex_t mutex;

void produce(int item) {
  sem_wait(&empty);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  buffer[in] = item;
  in = (in + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&full);
}

int consume() {
  int item;
  sem_wait(&full);
  pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);
  item = buffer[out];
  out = (out + 1) % N;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
  sem_post(&empty);
  return item;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multiple producers/consumers and ensure fairness?
- What are trade-offs vs. using condition variables instead of semaphores?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of practical scheduling design, aging to avoid starvation, and handling preemption in a simple OS.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-level feedback queue structure
- Quantum slicing and preemption
- Aging to prevent starvation
- Correctness under concurrent events and IO wakeups

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal MLFQ skeleton
const QUANTUM = [4,8,16];
let queues = [[], [], []];
function enqueue(t){ queues[t.level].push(t); }
function dequeue(){ for(let i=0;i<3;i++) if(queues[i].length) return queues[i].shift(); return null; }
function pick(){ let t = dequeue(); if(!t) return null; t.remaining = QUANTUM[t.level]; return t; }
```

